  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few spotty showers possible across the Southern \nTier through tonight, otherwise dry weather through the rest of the \nweek, with below normal temps returning to near normal by Saturday.\n\nOver the next few days the bulk of broad upper level troughing over \neastern Canada will slowly drift eastward and out to sea...while a \ndeveloping modest closed upper low drops southeastward from the \nUpper Midwest to the mid-Atlantic states. In between these two \nfeatures...upper-level ridging will build across Ontario and Quebec. \nMeanwhile at the surface...large Canadian high pressure will remain \ndraped from Ontario to Quebec and strengthen through Wednesday... \nthen will remain in place through the end of the week while a \nsurface low develops off the mid-Atlantic coastline in response to \nthe approaching cutoff upper low and attendant DCVA/height falls \naloft.\n\nGiven the above pattern evolution...about the only chance of \nprecipitation through the rest of the week will lie across the \nSouthern Tier today and tonight...along the far northern fringes of \nan inverted trough and deeper moisture situated over the Ohio \nValley. This could allow for a couple spotty light showers/sprinkles \nat times near the PA border through tonight/very early Wednesday... \nbefore the above mentioned closed low captures this system and draws \nit further away from our area during Wednesday. Otherwise the strong \nsurface high and initially confluent flow aloft should keep our \nregion dry through at least through Saturday...and possibly right \nthrough early next week should the aforementioned coastal system \nremain far enough to our east. \n\nOtherwise a persistent northeasterly to easterly low level flow \nacross our region will result in daytime highs only slowly warming \nthrough the rest of the week as our initially cool airmass gradually \nmodifies. Consequently...below normal highs ranging from the upper \n50s (higher terrain) to the lower to mid 60s (lower elevations) \ntoday will only slowly climb back to near normal/slightly below \nnormal levels in the mid 60s-lower 70s by Saturday. Meanwhile \nnighttime lows will remain largely below normal (mostly ranging \nthrough the 40s) through Friday night...with some upper 30s likely \nacross the North Country each of the next few nights...and also not \nout of the question across the interior of the Southern Tier both \nWednesday and Thursday nights. Coupled with mainly clear skies and \nlight winds...such readings may allow for at least some isolated \nfrost to develop across the North Country the next few nights... \nthough the areal coverage of such for tonight currently looks to be \nfar too low for any advisory.",
